plot_roc_curve\nsklearn/metrics/_plot/confusion_matrix.py:ConfusionMatrixDisplay.from_estimator\nsklearn/metrics/_plot/det_curve.py:plot_det_curve\n##Searching for term \"plot\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/det_curve.py:DetCurveDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/precision_recall_curve.py:PrecisionRecallDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py:RocCurveDisplay.plot`.\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/confusion_matrix.py:ConfusionMatrixDisplay.plot`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `plot`.\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"metrics/_plot\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/det_curve.py:DetCurveDisplay.from_estimator`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 84 | def from_estimator(\n 85 |         cls,\n 86 |         estimator,\n 87 |         X,\n 88 |         y,\n 89 |         *,\n 90 |         sample_weight=None,\n 91 |         response_method=\"auto\",\n 92 |         pos_label=None,\n 93 |         name=None,\n 94 |         ax=None,\n 95 |         **kwargs,\n 96 |     ):\n 97 |         \"\"\"Plot DET curve given an estimator and data.\n 98 | \n 99 |         Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <visualizations>`.\n100 | \n101 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n102 | \n103 |         Parameters\n104 |         ----------\n105 |         estimator : estimator instance\n106 |             Fitted classifier or a fitted :class:`~sklearn.pipeline.Pipeline`\n107 |             in which the last estimator is a classifier.\n108 | \n109 |         X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n110 |             Input values.\n111 | \n112 |         y : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n113 |             Target values.\n114 | \n115 |         sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n116 |             Sample weights.\n117 | \n118 |         response_method : {'predict_proba', 'decision_function', 'auto'} \\\n119 |                 default='auto'\n120 |             Specifies whether to use :term:`predict_proba` or\n121 |             :term:`decision_function` as the predicted target response. If set\n122 |             to 'auto', :term:`predict_proba` is tried first and if it does not\n123 |             exist :term:`decision_function` is tried next.\n124 | \n125 |         pos_label : str or int, default=None\n126 |             The label of the positive class. When `pos_label=None`, if `y_true`\n127 |             is in {-1, 1} or {0, 1}, `pos_label` is set to 1, otherwise an\n128 |             error will be raised.\n129 | \n130 |         name : str, default=None\n131 |             Name of DET curve for labeling. If `None`, use the name of the\n132 |             estimator.\n133 | \n134 |         ax : matplotlib axes, default=None\n135 |             Axes object to plot on. If `None`, a new figure and axes is\n136 |             created.\n137 | \n138 |         **kwargs : dict\n139 |             Additional keywords arguments passed to matplotlib `plot` function.\n140 | \n141 |         Returns\n142 |         -------\n143 |         display : :class:`~sklearn.metrics.DetCurveDisplay`\n144 |             Object that stores computed values.\n145 | \n146 |         See Also\n147 |         --------\n148 |         det_curve : Compute error rates for different probability thresholds.\n149 |         DetCurveDisplay.from_predictions : Plot DET curve given the true and\n150 |             predicted labels.\n151 |         plot_roc_curve : Plot Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve.\n152 | \n153 |         Examples\n154 |         --------\n155 |         >>> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n156 |         >>> from sklearn.datasets import make_classification\n157 |         >>> from sklearn.metrics import DetCurveDisplay\n158 |         >>> from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\n159 |         >>> from sklearn.svm import SVC\n160 |         >>> X, y = make_classification(n_samples=1000, random_state=0)\n161 |         >>> X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(\n162 |         ...     X, y, test_size=0.4, random_state=0)\n163 |         >>> clf = SVC(random_state=0).fit(X_train, y_train)\n164 |         >>> DetCurveDisplay.from_estimator(\n165 |         ...    clf, X_test, y_test)\n166 |         <...>\n167 |         >>> plt.show()\n168 |         \"\"\"\n169 |         check_matplotlib_support(f\"{cls.__name__}.from_estimator\")\n170 | \n171 |         name = estimator.__class__.__name__ if name is None else name\n172 | \n173 |         y_pred, pos_label = _get_response(\n174 |             X,\n175 |             estimator,\n176 |             response_method,\n177 |             pos_label=pos_label,\n178 |         )\n179 | \n180 |         return cls.from_predictions(\n181 |             y_true=y,\n182 |             y_pred=y_pred,\n183 |             sample_weight=sample_weight,\n184 |             name=name,\n185 |             ax=ax,\n186 |             pos_label=pos_label,\n187 |             **kwargs,\n188 |         )\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/metrics/_plot/roc_curve.py:plot_roc_curve`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n134 | def plot_roc_curve(\n135 |     estimator,\n136 |     X,\n137 |     y,\n138 |     *,\n139 |     sample_weight=None,\n140 |     drop_intermediate=True,\n141 |     response_method=\"auto\",\n142 |     name=None,\n143 |     ax=None,\n144 |     pos_label=None,\n145 |     **kwargs,\n146 | ):\n147 |     \"\"\"Plot Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve.\n148 | \n149 |     Extra keyword arguments will be passed to matplotlib's `plot`.\n150 | \n151 |     Read more in the :ref:`User Guide <visualizations>`.\n152 | \n153 |     Parameters\n154 |     ----------\n155 |     estimator : estimator instance\n156 |         Fitted classifier or a fitted :class:`~sklearn.pipeline.Pipeline`\n157 |         in which the last estimator is a classifier.\n158 | \n159 |     X : {array-like, sparse matrix} of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n160 |         Input values.\n161 | \n162 |     y : array-like of shape (n_samples,)\n163 |         Target values.\n164 | \n165 |     sample_weight : array-like of shape (n_samples,), default=None\n166 |         Sample weights.\n167 | \n168 |     drop_intermediate : boolean, default=True\n169 |         Whether to drop some suboptimal thresholds which would not appear\n170 |         on a plotted ROC curve. This is useful in order to create lighter\n171 |         ROC curves.\n172 | \n173 |     response_method : {'predict_proba', 'decision_function', 'auto'} \\\n174 |     default='auto'\n175 |         Specifies whether to use :term:`predict_proba` or\n176 |         :term:`decision_function` as the target response. If set to 'auto',\n177 |         :term:`predict_proba` is tried first and if it does not exist\n178 |         :term:`decision_function` is tried next.\n179 | \n180 |     name : str, default=None\n181 |         Name of ROC Curve for labeling. If `None`, use the name of the\n182 |         estimator.\n183 | \n184 |     ax : matplotlib axes, default=None\n185 |         Axes object to plot on. If `None`, a new figure and axes is created.\n186 | \n187 |     pos_label : str or int, default=None\n188 |         The class considered as the positive class when computing the roc auc\n189 |         metrics. By default, `estimators.classes_[1]` is considered\n190 |         as the positive class.\n191 | \n192 |         .. versionadded:: 0.24\n193 | \n194 |     Returns\n195 |     -------\n196 |     display : :class:`~sklearn.metrics.RocCurveDisplay`\n197 |         Object that stores computed values.\n198 | \n199 |     See Also\n200 |     --------\n201 |     roc_curve : Compute Receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve.\n202 |     RocCurveDisplay : ROC Curve visualization.\n203 |     roc_auc_score : Compute the area under the ROC curve.\n204 | \n205 |     Examples\n206 |  