6 | \n1857 |     The plotting system of pandas uses matplotlib by default, but the idea here\n1858 |     is that it can also work with other third-party backends. This function\n1859 |     returns the module which provides a top-level `.plot` method that will\n1860 |     actually do the plotting. The backend is specified from a string, which\n1861 |     either comes from the keyword argument `backend`, or, if not specified, from\n1862 |     the option `pandas.options.plotting.backend`. All the rest of the code in\n1863 |     this file uses the backend specified there for the plotting.\n1864 | \n1865 |     The backend is imported lazily, as matplotlib is a soft dependency, and\n1866 |     pandas can be used without it being installed.\n1867 | \n1868 |     Notes\n1869 |     -----\n1870 |     Modifies `_backends` with imported backend as a side effect.\n1871 |     \"\"\"\n1872 |     backend = backend or get_option(\"plotting.backend\")\n1873 | \n1874 |     if backend in _backends:\n1875 |         return _backends[backend]\n1876 | \n1877 |     module = _load_backend(backend)\n1878 |     _backends[backend] = module\n1879 |     return module\n```\n\nFound file `pandas/plotting/_core.py`.\nFound file `pandas/plotting/__init__.py`.\nFound file `pandas/plotting/_misc.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"plot backend\", \"plot backend figure generation subplot layout\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound file `asv_bench/benchmarks/plotting.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Search `asv_bench/benchmarks/plotting.py` for the full content if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"plot backend\", \"subplot layout\", \"plot backend figure generation subplot layout\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_core.py:boxplot_frame_groupby`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n526 | def boxplot_frame_groupby(\n527 |     grouped,\n528 |     subplots=True,\n529 |     column=None,\n530 |     fontsize=None,\n531 |     rot=0,\n532 |     grid=True,\n533 |     ax=None,\n534 |     figsize=None,\n535 |     layout=None,\n536 |     sharex=False,\n537 |     sharey=True,\n538 |     backend=None,\n539 |     **kwargs,\n540 | ):\n541 |     \"\"\"\n542 |     Make box plots from DataFrameGroupBy data.\n543 | \n544 |     Parameters\n545 |     ----------\n546 |     grouped : Grouped DataFrame\n547 |     subplots : bool\n548 |         * ``False`` - no subplots will be used\n549 |         * ``True`` - create a subplot for each group.\n550 | \n551 |     column : column name or list of names, or vector\n552 |         Can be any valid input to groupby.\n553 |     fontsize : int or str\n554 |     rot : label rotation angle\n555 |     grid : Setting this to True will show the grid\n556 |     ax : Matplotlib axis object, default None\n557 |     figsize : A tuple (width, height) in inches\n558 |     layout : tuple (optional)\n559 |         The layout of the plot: (rows, columns).\n560 |     sharex : bool, default False\n561 |         Whether x-axes will be shared among subplots.\n562 |     sharey : bool, default True\n563 |         Whether y-axes will be shared among subplots.\n564 |     backend : str, default None\n565 |         Backend to use instead of the backend specified in the option\n566 |         ``plotting.backend``. For instance, 'matplotlib'. Alternatively, to\n567 |         specify the ``plotting.backend`` for the whole session, set\n568 |         ``pd.options.plotting.backend``.\n569 | \n570 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0.0\n571 | \n572 |     **kwargs\n573 |         All other plotting keyword arguments to be passed to\n574 |         matplotlib's boxplot function.\n575 | \n576 |     Returns\n577 |     -------\n578 |     dict of key/value = group key/DataFrame.boxplot return value\n579 |     or DataFrame.boxplot return value in case subplots=figures=False\n580 | \n581 |     Examples\n582 |     --------\n583 |     You can create boxplots for grouped data and show them as separate subplots:\n584 | \n585 |     .. plot::\n586 |         :context: close-figs\n587 | \n588 |         >>> import itertools\n589 |         >>> tuples = [t for t in itertools.product(range(1000), range(4))]\n590 |         >>> index = pd.MultiIndex.from_tuples(tuples, names=['lvl0', 'lvl1'])\n591 |         >>> data = np.random.randn(len(index),4)\n592 |         >>> df = pd.DataFrame(data, columns=list('ABCD'), index=index)\n593 |         >>> grouped = df.groupby(level='lvl1')\n594 |         >>> grouped.boxplot(rot=45, fontsize=12, figsize=(8,10))  # doctest: +SKIP\n595 | \n596 |     The ``subplots=False`` option shows the boxplots in a single figure.\n597 | \n598 |     .. plot::\n599 |         :context: close-figs\n600 | \n601 |         >>> grouped.boxplot(subplots=False, rot=45, fontsize=12)  # doctest: +SKIP\n602 |     \"\"\"\n603 |     plot_backend = _get_plot_backend(backend)\n604 |     return plot_backend.boxplot_frame_groupby(\n605 |         grouped,\n606 |         subplots=subplots,\n607 |         column=column,\n608 |         fontsize=fontsize,\n609 |         rot=rot,\n610 |         grid=grid,\n611 |         ax=ax,\n612 |         figsize=figsize,\n613 |         layout=layout,\n614 |         sharex=sharex,\n615 |         sharey=sharey,\n616 |         **kwargs,\n617 |     )\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"figure generation\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py:do_adjust_figure`, `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py:maybe_adjust_figure`, `pandas/plotting/_matplotlib/tools.py:format_date_labels`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | # being a bit too dynamic\n  2 | from __future__ import annotations\n  3 | \n  4 | from math import ceil\n  5 | from typing import (\n  6 |     TYPE_CHECKING,\n  7 |     Iterable,\n  8 |     Sequence,\n  9 | )\n 10 | import warnings\n 11 | \n 12 | import m